## 4. Robustness Analysis

### 4.1 Final Fitness Distribution Across Seeds

Robustness measures the consistency of achieving near-optimal solutions across different random seeds. This section analyzes best fitness distributions (100 seeds per pipeline per landscape) to identify which algorithms maintain stable performance despite stochastic variation.

#### Robustness Metrics per Landscape

| Landscape | Pipeline | Mean Best Fitness | Stdev | Robustness* |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Sphere 10D** | evosax_differential_evolution | -193.8567 | 0.0000 | **∞** |
| | evosax_simplega | -191.2050 | 0.6622 | 288.7 |
| | malthusjax_tournament | 193.8566 | 0.0001 | 2,170,000+ |
| | malthusjax_default | 193.8558 | 0.0004 | 483,000+ |
| | malthusjax_roulette | 193.8005 | 0.0170 | 11,400 |
| **Sphere 20D** | evosax_differential_evolution | -193.8567 | 0.0000054 | 36M+ |
| | evosax_simplega | -165.3319 | 4.3252 | **38.2** (WORST) |
| | malthusjax_tournament | 193.8467 | 0.0028 | 69,000+ |
| | malthusjax_default | 193.8421 | 0.0032 | 60,600+ |
| | malthusjax_roulette | 193.6871 | 0.0371 | 5,200 |
| **Ellipsoidal 10D** (ill-conditioned) | malthusjax_default | -2440.0264 | 1802.16 | 1.35 |
| | malthusjax_tournament | -2229.0379 | 1542.87 | 1.44 |
| | malthusjax_roulette | -408.5993 | **53.12** (BEST) | 7.69 |
| | evosax_differential_evolution | -193.8566 | 0.0001 | 1.9M+ |
| | evosax_simplega | 28859.6617 | 9981.23 | **-2.89** (poor robustness) |
| **Rosenbrock 10D** (valley) | evosax_differential_evolution | -193.6574 | 0.8732 | 222.0 |
| | malthusjax_roulette | 187.9286 | **0.3613** (BEST) | 520.2 |
| | malthusjax_default | 188.2998 | 0.3719 | 505.7 |
| | malthusjax_tournament | 188.4353 | 0.6245 | 301.3 |
| | evosax_simplega | 141.4200 | 98.6743 | **1.43** (WORST) |

*Robustness = |Mean/Stdev| (higher indicates more consistent results; lower indicates seed-dependent variance)

### 4.2 Key Robustness Findings

#### Ultra-Robust Performers (Stdev < 0.001)
- **evosax_differential_evolution** on Sphere problems (Stdev ≈ 0): DE algorithm finds identical optimum across all 100 seeds—perfect deterministic behavior despite PRNG variation
- **MalthusJAX variants** on Sphere 10D (Stdev 10⁻⁴ range): Elite-pool and tournament selection converge to near-identical solutions with ultra-tight distributions

**Interpretation**: Simple, unimodal landscapes permit perfect trajectories regardless of initial random state. Algorithm design matters less than landscape structure here.

#### Landscape-Dependent Robustness Inversion

**Sphere problems** (convex, simple):
- **Ranking by Robustness**: DE >> MalthusJAX elite/tournament (10M+ ratio) >> Roulette >> SimpleGA
- **Pattern**: Greedy elitism produces deterministic convergence; diversity-promoting roulette introduces seed-dependent variance

**Ellipsoidal 10D** (ill-conditioned, multiple plateaus):
- **Ranking by Robustness**: Roulette >> Default ≈ Tournament (5.36× better than elite pool)
- **Selection reversal**: Roulette becomes most robust on ill-conditioned problems (mean -408.6, stdev 53.1) vs elite pool (mean -2440.0, stdev 1802.2)
- **Interpretation**: Ill-conditioned landscapes benefit from diversity (roulette) because greedy pathways lead to **seed-dependent trapping** in ravines. Roulette's stochastic selection avoids systematic convergence failures.

**Rosenbrock 10D** (curved valley):
- **Ranking by Robustness**: Roulette >> Default ≈ Tournament >> DE >> SimpleGA
- **Pattern**: MalthusJAX variants (esp. Roulette) maintain tight control (stdev 0.36–0.62) while SimpleGA shows high variance (98.67), indicating seed-dependent valley-finding success

#### SimpleGA Performance Limitations
- **Sphere 20D**: Stdev increase 0.66 → 4.33 (6.5× increase from 10D), Mean decreases -191.2 → -165.3
- **Ellipsoidal 10D**: Stdev 9981.2, Mean 28859.7 (wrong fitness basin entirely)
- **Rosenbrock 10D**: Mean 141.4, far right of feasible region
- **Interpretation**: SimpleGA lacks adaptive mutation and relies on population diversity; higher dimensions and non-convex landscapes expose this limitation across multiple random seeds

### 4.3 Robustness as Practical Selection Metric

For practitioners choosing algorithms, robustness reveals **algorithm reliability**:

| Metric | Best Pipeline | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| **Lowest variance (most consistent)** | malthusjax_roulette (Ellipsoidal: 53.1) | Ill-conditioned problems: prioritize consistency |
| **Zero variance (deterministic)** | evosax_differential_evolution (Sphere) | Reproducibility-critical applications |
| **High mean + low variance (balanced)** | malthusjax_tournament (Rosenbrock: 188.44 ± 0.62) | General-purpose: good solutions, reliable |

**Recommendation**: For practical applications, prioritize **robustness (low seed-dependent variance) over mean fitness**, as a reliable algorithm delivering consistent solutions is preferable to one with high mean but high variance, which may fail unexpectedly on some seeds.

